# fleet.manifest — the DECLARATION of every launchd job this repo owns (DAEMON_FLEET_V2 §4.1).
#
# One record per line, `#` comments, `|`-separated, six fields:
#
#   label | expect | interval_s | evidence | owner_row | activate
#
#   label       the launchd label, exactly as it appears in `launchctl print-disabled gui/<uid>`.
#   expect      run | staged | retired — what this box is SUPPOSED to be doing with the job:
#                 run      expected live NOW. bin/cc-fleet runs the full state function over it
#                          (S1 NOT-INSTALLED → S2 DISABLED → S3 NEVER-RAN → S4 FAILING →
#                          S5 STALLED → S6 HEALTHY, first match wins) and emits a row unless S6.
#                 staged   BUILT, activation decision PENDING. Always emits exactly ONE row,
#                          state=UNDECIDED — "declared, decision pending: surfaced, never silent".
#                          It is deliberately NOT a daemon-fault row: nothing is broken, an
#                          operator decision is outstanding (activate it, or declare it retired).
#                          This is the honest answer where the evidence does not settle whether
#                          the job is supposed to be running; ambiguity is DECLARED, never
#                          resolved by an agent guessing an alarm (noise) or a silence (the
#                          failure this whole file exists to end).
#                 retired  deliberately not running. NEVER a row — retired is not broken (R8).
#   interval_s  the DECLARED cadence in seconds. `0` for calendar-scheduled jobs (StartCalendar-
#               Interval / RunAtLoad-only), whose staleness bound is 86400 x CC_FLEET_STALE_FACTOR.
#   evidence    the durable product that proves execution (R4). One of:
#                 auto     read StandardOutPath out of the LIVE plist. PREFER THIS — it is
#                          self-maintaining: change the plist and the sensor follows.
#                 <path>   an explicit path; a leading `~/` is expanded at read time.
#                 -        no artifact exists. Then only launchd's own counters decide S3/S4 and
#                          S5 is NEVER claimed (R8: no sensor, no verdict — not a red). A plist
#                          with no StandardOutPath key resolves `auto` to exactly this.
#   owner_row   the GROUND_UP_REBUILD_MAP row that answers when this breaks. Routing metadata
#               only — it never changes a verdict.
#   activate    the pending-activation script that turns the job on, filename only; it becomes the
#               row's paste-ready recover_cmd. The C10 boundary: agents drive TO it, never run it.
#   ok_exits    OPTIONAL 7th field. Comma-separated exit codes this job uses to express a HEALTHY
#               outcome; absent or malformed ⇒ `0`. A job whose DESIGNED verdict is a non-zero
#               refusal is NOT failing: `com.claude.deploy-live` exits **1** to mean "no GREEN
#               stamp — nothing is safe to deploy" (verify: `deploy-live.sh --dry-run`). Keying S4
#               on `exit != 0` alone would put a permanent, unfixable row on a healthy job, which
#               is the alarm-fatigue failure this whole file exists to prevent (plan §5 F20).
#
# WHY FOUR ROWS BELOW CARRY `evidence = -` RATHER THAN `auto` (measured 2026-07-29, plan §5 F20):
# S5 STALLED compares an artifact's mtime against the declared cadence, so it needs a durable
# product the job touches on EVERY run. postland-verify, deploy-live, dispatcher and discovery do
# not have one: deploy-live is damped-silent on refusal (it deliberately writes nothing for a whole
# day), `idl.jsonl` is a SHARED journal so its mtime does not prove the dispatcher specifically ran,
# and one postland-verify corpus cycle takes ~50 min against a 300s interval so evidence-age
# bounded by interval x factor can never be right for it. Declaring `-` is R8 applied honestly —
# no sensor ⇒ NO VERDICT, not a red. They keep full S1/S2/S3/S4 coverage from launchd's own
# counters, which is where their real failures live. The principled upgrade that WOULD make S5
# provable for them is a persisted `runs` cursor per label (launchd increments `runs` on every
# invocation regardless of output); that is named in the plan as the next step, NOT done here —
# and a silent false STALLED would have been worse than an honest gap.
#
# A lint (§4.4) enforces manifest <-> launchd/*.plist <-> ~/Library/LaunchAgents three-way
# coverage, so a new plist cannot land undeclared. `bin/cc-fleet --plist-parity` is that read.
#
# ── THE THREE WATCHERS, and why only ONE is declared `run` (audit 2026-07-22 V6) ────────────────────
# V6: "watcher daemons have no launchd KeepAlive — 'absence is the alarm' has no reader". The supervisor,
# reconciler and deathwatch each write a heartbeat whose ABSENCE should page, and nothing was reading it.
# This file IS that reader (cc-fleet's S3/S5 arms), so a watcher enters coverage by being declared here.
# The three are NOT interchangeable, and pinning them identically would have been actively wrong:
#
#   lead-supervisor   PERSISTENT (`--daemon`, an internal sweep loop) ⇒ KeepAlive is exactly right, and
#                     its plist already carried it. Declared `run` here: it is built, its e2e is green,
#                     and the only thing ever missing was a declaration that it SHOULD be up.
#                     Its evidence is supervisor.log, NOT `auto`: `auto` resolves to the plist's
#                     StandardOutPath (supervisor.out.log), and the daemon redirects every line it writes
#                     to files — stdout stays 0 bytes even when perfectly healthy (measured: 0 bytes
#                     since Jul 14 vs a 1.2MB supervisor.log), so `auto` is a sensor that could only ever
#                     report STALLED. supervisor.log gets a heartbeat line every sweep.
#
#   lead-reconciler   PERIODIC (`--once`, no loop). KeepAlive on a one-shot is a 10-second relaunch
#                     THRASH forever, so the correct pin is StartInterval — see
#                     launchd/staged/com.claude.lead-reconciler.plist. It stays STAGED (hence out of this
#                     manifest, per the staged/ convention) behind ONE named gate: roster_tasks is
#                     deliberately unwired (lead-reconciler.sh:50-56 — "the operator supplies the
#                     task-table reader at activation. Empty by default so a mis-deploy diverges
#                     LOUDLY"). An empty roster A makes every live pid "in registry, ABSENT from tasks".
#                     MEASURED (2 seeded live pids, GRACE_S=0, production roster defaults): 2 alarms on
#                     tasks-x-registry AND 2 on registry-x-disk — because roster C comes from the
#                     deathwatch heartbeat, which is not running either, so BOTH empty rosters compound.
#                     Scaled to this box's ~30 live sessions that is ~60 one-shot alarms, not ~30. GATE:
#                     set CC_RECON_ROSTER_TASKS in that plist, then declare it `run` here. Note the two
#                     gates are coupled — roster C stays empty until deathwatch has a producer, so a
#                     reconciler activated alone still alarms on registry-x-disk for every live session.
#
#   lead-deathwatch   NOT pinned, and deliberately NOT given a plist. Both its modes need a watch-file,
#                     and NOTHING in production writes one (only bin/cc-deathwatch-kqueue's docs and the
#                     tests reference the format). `--watch` DIES on a missing file, which under
#                     KeepAlive is that same 10s thrash; `--once` tolerates it but then watches an empty
#                     list forever — ~100% abstain, i.e. inert BY CONSTRUCTION, the one thing a mechanism
#                     must never be. GATE: a production watch-file producer (the P8-registry → watch-list
#                     path its own header describes as composition) has to exist first. Declared here in
#                     words rather than as a row, because a row needs a plist and a plist here would
#                     claim a coverage that does not exist.
#
# label | expect | interval_s | evidence | owner_row | activate
com.claude.postland-verify        | run    | 300  | -    | 1  | 14-land-pipeline-v2-activate.sh
com.claude.deploy-live            | run    | 600  | -    | 1  | 14-land-pipeline-v2-activate.sh | 0,1
com.claude.dispatcher             | run    | 900  | -    | 5  | 02-load-dispatcher-activate.sh
com.claude.discovery              | run    | 3600 | -    | 5  | 03-load-discovery-activate.sh
com.claude.log-rotation           | run    | 3600 | auto | 12 | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.session-search-sweep   | run    | 60   | auto | 9  | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.session-search-backfill| run    | 0    | auto | 9  | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.caffeinate-floor       | staged | 3600 | auto | 12 | 05-pmset-caffeinate-activate.sh
com.claude.power-policy-verify    | staged | 3600 | auto | 12 | 05-pmset-caffeinate-activate.sh
com.claude.team-orphan-reaper     | staged | 600  | auto | 11 | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.desk-invariant         | staged | 300  | auto | 5  | 06-desk-bootstrap-activate.sh
com.claude.boot-resume            | staged | 300  | auto | 2  | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.lead-supervisor        | run    | 0    | ~/.claude/autonomy/supervisor.log | 2 | 18-fleet-activate.sh
com.claude.nightly-regression     | staged | 0    | auto | 1  | 18-fleet-activate.sh
# Declared here to repair the three-way coverage lint, which commit 12595000 landed RED on the trunk:
# it added launchd/com.claude.capacity-alarm.plist with no manifest row, so `cc-fleet --plist-parity` and
# tests/cc-fleet.bats ("declares every repo plist") failed on pristine origin/main — not for any lander
# who came after. Declared `staged`, from disk truth, because the job is neither installed in
# ~/Library/LaunchAgents nor loaded: an activation decision is outstanding, which is exactly what
# `staged` says (ONE UNDECIDED row, never a guessed alarm). interval 600 = its StartInterval; `auto`
# resolves to its StandardOutPath and the script does write to stdout, so unlike lead-supervisor's the
# sensor is real. The job's owner may flip this to `run` after activating it.
# FLIPPED staged→run 2026-07-30 (row 13 completion): the activation RAN — 19-capacity-alarm-activate.sh.done
# exists (Jul 29 19:09), `launchctl print gui/501/com.claude.capacity-alarm` shows enabled + 20 runs +
# last exit 0, and ~/.claude/logs/capacity-alarm.jsonl accrues rows every ~600 s. The `staged` row had
# become factually false and made the parity leg assert a state the box left a day earlier.
com.claude.capacity-alarm         | run    | 600  | auto | 13 | 19-capacity-alarm-activate.sh
# ── com.claude.relogin (row 7, ACCOUNT_ROUTING_V2 M4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY IT WAS MISSING, and why that mattered: this label was invisible to the three-way coverage loop
# because that loop globs launchd/*.plist and this plist lives in launchd/staged/ — deliberately, so
# install.sh's glob cannot auto-activate credentials automation (8a1e49ab: "structure, not a
# conditional"). Net effect: a poller that has existed and been tested since 2026-07-26 has NEVER been
# scheduled — TWO log lines all-time, both from a hand invocation — and cc-fleet never evaluated it, so
# NOTHING said so. That is feature-durability-mechanism-not-memory, doubled: inert AND unreportable.
# Declared here so the inertness is READABLE (existence evidence comes from the DECLARATION, not from
# the subject's success).
#
# `staged`, from disk truth: not in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, not loaded. Exactly ONE UNDECIDED row —
# "declared, decision pending" — never a daemon-fault row, because nothing is broken; an operator
# decision is outstanding. Row 7's owner flips this to `run` after activating.
#
# evidence is the PER-TICK artifact, NOT `auto`: the plist's StandardOutPath is cc-relogin-poll.out.log,
# but the poller writes every line through its own log() to cc-relogin-poll.log and the launchd args
# carry no --json, so stdout stays ~0 bytes even when healthy — `auto` here would be a sensor that can
# only ever say STALLED (lead-supervisor's lesson, verified the same way). Every terminal path of a
# scheduled tick logs (nothing-due, WINDOW-CAPPED, DETECTION-UNAVAILABLE, SKIP, ATTEMPT, ESCALATED), so
# mtime-vs-cadence is a real S5 sensor for this file.
#
# ok_exits 0,5: exit 5 is ESCALATED — the DESIGNED loud verdict at T-48h, raising the class-C
# relogin-blocked row. Keying S4 on exit != 0 would put a permanent, unfixable FAILING row on a job
# doing precisely its job (plan §5 F20, deploy-live's exit 1 is the same shape). Exit 3
# (DETECTION-UNAVAILABLE) is deliberately NOT ok — "cannot tell whether a re-login is due" must row.
com.claude.relogin                | staged | 3600 | ~/.claude/logs/cc-relogin-poll.log | 7 | 21-relogin-poll-activate.sh | 0,5
# ── com.claude.scratchpad-reaper — a TRUNK REPAIR, not row 7's artifact ────────────────────────────
# `97d4984b feat(growth): scratchpad-reaper for the /private/tmp per-session tree` landed
# launchd/com.claude.scratchpad-reaper.plist with NO manifest row, which puts BOTH the three-way
# coverage lint and tests/cc-fleet.bats "declares every repo plist" RED on pristine origin/main —
# for every lander who comes after, not for its author. Same failure and same repair as
# com.claude.capacity-alarm above, so it takes the same treatment rather than an allowlist.
# All four fields are DISK TRUTH, read not assumed: `staged` because it is neither in
# ~/Library/LaunchAgents nor loaded (`launchctl print` → not found); interval 21600 = its
# StartInterval; `auto` is a REAL sensor here, unlike com.claude.relogin's — scripts/
# scratchpad-reaper.sh:157 echoes a per-run summary to stdout, so its StandardOutPath is written on
# every run. owner_row 11 (worktree/scratchpad disk hygiene) is the best fit and is PROVISIONAL: the
# field is routing metadata only and never changes a verdict, so its real owner may correct it
# without touching anything else. activate = the generic fleet activation, as boot-resume,
# nightly-regression and team-orphan-reaper already do — no scratchpad-specific script exists.
com.claude.scratchpad-reaper      | staged | 21600 | auto | 11 | 18-fleet-activate.sh

# com.claude.qos-census — row 13 completion (M12): the QoS coverage claim decays without a cadence
# (every qos-census.jsonl row before 2026-07-30 was a manual run). Same shape as capacity-alarm:
# 600 s, Background band. NO-BURST ticks (exit 3) are cheap, honest non-verdicts. Staged until the
# operator activates via 23-qos-census-activate.sh.
com.claude.qos-census             | staged | 600  | auto | 13 | 23-qos-census-activate.sh

